  "title": "India's Fiscal Deficit Widens Amid Rapid Capital Expenditure and Subsidies",
  "summary": "India's fiscal deficit widened to ₹7.10 trillion during April-August, driven by significant increases in capital expenditure and major subsidies, with the deficit representing 41.9% of the full-year target. The World Bank raises India's FY27 growth forecast to 7.1% from 6.6%, but risks from high oil prices, El Niño, and capital flow volatility are highlighted.",
  "facts": [
    {
      "statement": "India's fiscal deficit widened to ₹7.10 trillion during April-August 2027, representing 41.9% of the full-year target.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "The deficit is attributed to increased capital expenditure and major subsidies, with the World Bank raising the growth forecast to 7.1%.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "statement": "The government data shows a rise in capital expenditure to ₹6.5 trillion in the first half of FY27.",
      "confidence": 0.95
